# T-3551 (SEV-1 T-3549, G-019) — Environment-leak canary
# Source of truth: /opt/termlink/.context/cron/env-leak-canary.crontab (git-tracked)
# Installed to:    /etc/cron.d/termlink-env-leak-canary (USER field syntax — do NOT load into a user crontab)
#
# On 2026-10-22 the host's default tmux server was seeded with another project's
# TASKS_DIR/CONTEXT_DIR and 30 of 54 registered sessions ran with them; it was found
# by hand. FIRES when a registered session's root process carries a project variable
# that points into a different project than the one the session works in, or when
# the default tmux server's global environment holds any project variable at all.
# Empty log = healthy. /canaries auto-discovers this log; tooling errors go to the
# .stderr sink (T-2842).
#
# Hourly, not daily: a leaked environment acts on the wrong project's tasks for as
# long as it goes unnoticed.
#
# Operator action on firing: relaunch the named sessions from a clean shell (another
# project's agents are that project's or the operator's call); for a server global,
# `tmux set-environment -gu <VAR>`. Detail: bash scripts/check-env-leak-freshness.sh
SHELL=/bin/bash
TERMLINK_WORKER_CLASS=background
PATH=/root/.cargo/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin

# Hourly at :17 — termlink from /root/.cargo/bin first (the current build; see arc-claim-drift note).
17 * * * * root cd /opt/termlink && bash scripts/check-env-leak-freshness.sh --quiet >> .context/working/.env-leak-canary.log 2>> .context/working/.env-leak-canary.log.stderr
